3️⃣ What Actually Happened

Describe reality, not intent.

Timeline of Failure

What was attempted?

What happened?

When did the first warning appear?

When did it become unrecoverable?

4️⃣ Root Cause Analysis

Root cause is not “stuff went wrong.”
Root cause is the thing that, if fixed, would have prevented failure.

Technical Root Causes

Hardware?

Physics?

Materials?

System behavior?

Architectural Root Causes

Wrong assumptions?

Bad abstractions?

Wrong mission framing?

Overconfidence in cleverness?

Organizational / Governance Root Causes

Did someone ignore Phase 2?

Was drift allowed?

Were reports honest?

Did leadership push stupidity?

Was independence compromised?

License / Discipline Root Causes

Did anyone execute outside authorized scope?

Did anyone cut reality corners?

Do not pretend “it was complicated.” Complication is not a cause. It’s camouflage.

5️⃣ Were Kill Conditions Triggered?

Phase 3 defines conditions where execution must stop.

Did kill conditions occur?
☐ Yes
☐ No

If yes:

When?

Was the Stop enforced?

If not enforced, explain why — bluntly.

If no, explain why failure still occurred despite not technically hitting kill rules.

6️⃣ Concept Drift Examination

Was failure caused or amplified by drift?

☐ No drift — system failed honestly

☐ Drift present and corrected

☐ Drift present and tolerated

☐ Drift directly caused failure

Explain:

What drift looked like, who allowed it, and why.

If drift existed and governance didn’t stop it, governance failed.

7️⃣ Phase Alignment Autopsy

Did execution stay true to earlier phases?

Phase 0 Purpose honored?

☐ Yes ☐ No

Phase 1 scope discipline respected?

☐ Yes ☐ No

Phase 2 constraints and risks respected?

☐ Yes ☐ No

If No anywhere, explain how ignoring those contributed to failure.

8️⃣ Integrity & Honesty Assessment

Failure becomes disaster when people lie.

Answer bluntly:

Were engineers pressured to soften reality?

Did managers hide risk?

Did executives force optimism?

Did independence collapse?

Did anyone pretend “success” longer than reality justified?

If yes, document. Names optional. Behavior mandatory.

9️⃣ What Was Learned

Lessons must be:

actionable

specific

non-vague

non-corporate

Examples of bad lessons:

“We need better communication.”

“We should be more careful.”

“We learned a lot.”

Examples of acceptable lessons:

“Assumption X is false in real environments.”

“Architecture must include Y or it collapses.”

“Institutional drift controls were insufficient.”

“Never rely on Z as a stable dependency again.”

